#
# The token a `$( … )` body's refusal names — #3296.
#
# The body is not a program on its own: in the script it is followed by the
# parenthesis that closed it. A shell that reads the body separately runs out
# of *input* where the script's own reader meets a `)`, and each of them has
# its own rule for what running out of input means — so the token named was
# the right rule answering the wrong question. Here it was `end of file
# unexpected (expecting "then")`, where dash names the closer.
#
# **This column was recorded as already correct**, and it was correct on one
# construct. Measured 2026-09-16 over ten bodies of the form `v=$(echo hi; X)`,
# BusyBox names `unexpected ")"` for nine of them and writes its own sentence
# for exactly one — a bare `for`, whose loop variable it judges as a name
# whatever stands in the place, however impossible a name that thing is.
# #3296's table measured `for`, found this shell byte-perfect, and recorded it
# as right. It was right on the single construct every shell on the panel
# treats specially.
#
# A copy of dash/substitution-closer.tests rather than a shared file, because
# this column's reference is BusyBox inside a container and dash's is
# /bin/dash out here: one file graded twice would be one measurement claimed
# for two shells. The two sentences are **not** the same — `syntax error:
# unexpected ")"` here against `Syntax error: ")" unexpected` there — so the
# file says so twice rather than assuming once, which is the failure #3248 and
# the five after it each were.
#
# One failing case, and it is last, for the reason
# ash/substitution-fatality.tests gives: this failure ends the script here,
# and a subshell does not contain it. The controls above it all parse.

printf 'the controls first\n'

# A body that parses, which is what says the body is read at all.
v=$(echo hi); printf 'an ordinary body -> [%s]\n' "$v"

# A body whose grammar fails *before* the closer is reached. The refusal is
# the body's own and this change must not move it — but it is a control here
# rather than a case, because it is fatal too: it is written so that it never
# fails, and `share/suite/core` grades the shape that does.
w=$(echo hi; echo x); printf 'two commands -> [%s]\n' "$w"

# The older spelling, which ends at its own backquote rather than where its
# contents end, and which the columns read with the script. It is the control
# on the other side: this change is the parenthesised form's alone, and a
# backquoted body that parses proves the form still works at all.
x=`echo hi`; printf 'the older spelling -> [%s]\n' "$x"

# And the case. `if` rather than `for`, because a bare `for` is the one
# construct the whole panel answers specially — dash and BusyBox call the
# closer a bad loop variable rather than an unexpected token, so it is the
# one shape that cannot show this.
printf 'the case\n'
v=$(echo hi; if)
printf 'never reached\n'
